WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
CSRFs used to be a really popular bug class,

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:08.220
but then browser developers did something really smart

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.140
and they introduced a cookie attribute called SameSite

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:16.139
that was supposed to prevent these vulnerabilities.

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:18.500
But did it really kill CSRFs?

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.500
Can you still get good bounties for CSRFs in 2024?

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.700
Well, there's only one way to find out,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.639
to do the case study, and so I did.

00:00:29.639 --> 00:00:32.599
In this video, I will tell you what techniques

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:36.560
still work in 2024 and how you can still get

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.880
good bounties for CSRFs.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:39.720
Enjoy.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.720
And the first label that I have in this category

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.040
is very optimistic for the future of CSRFs

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:53.040
because in 38 reports, I decided that the SameSite cookie

00:00:53.959 --> 00:00:56.840
would not, SameSite lags to be more precise,

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.680
would not help because the request with CSRF

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.919
was using the get method.

00:01:03.919 --> 00:01:07.000
And there were a few different types of attacks here,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.959
different types of get requests.

00:01:09.959 --> 00:01:13.400
I broke this down in the other part of the case study

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.959
that's in BBRE Premium Archive, but for example,

00:01:16.959 --> 00:01:21.080
it was a report like this one in Facebook

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.959
where the hunter would send a get request,

00:01:25.599 --> 00:01:30.080
that wouldn't directly make any modifications on the server,

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.839
but it would cause the client-side JavaScript

00:01:33.839 --> 00:01:36.720
to immediately, without any user interaction,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.959
send the request to the server.

00:01:39.959 --> 00:01:43.120
And this request done by JavaScript would be a POST request

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.779
and it would have the proper CSRF token and everything,

00:01:46.779 --> 00:01:51.480
but the attacker would control the path of this request

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.760
and potentially parameters as well.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:57.040
So the attacker would control everything,

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.440
the client-side JavaScript would automatically append

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.480
the CSRF token.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:07.480
And in this case, SameSite attribute with the lag setting

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.500
or with the strict, to be honest as well, does not help you.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.520
And this report, it was submitted in 2022

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.440
when SameSite was already working.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.360
It was $18,750.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:23.559
So quite a good report.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.279
We see that Facebook, I think, has problems

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:29.600
with these types of CSRFs because,

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.559
take a look at how many Facebook reports we have here.

00:02:32.559 --> 00:02:36.720
That's eight reports, a few of them in five-digit bounties.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.800
So quite an important backlash.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.679
The next category that I found here

00:02:45.679 --> 00:02:48.800
was before SameSite defaulting to lags,

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.800
because if we take a look at the CSRF reports by year,

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.800
SameSite attribute was introduced in Chrome back in 2016,

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.619
but for four years, it was pretty irrelevant

00:03:04.619 --> 00:03:08.039
because only Chrome supported it

00:03:08.039 --> 00:03:12.600
and also developers had to actively implement this,

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.820
which developers are not really eager

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.000
to implement new defense mechanisms

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.880
until they have to.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:23.919
And it makes a lot of sense from the business perspective.

00:03:23.919 --> 00:03:27.679
And in this period, you couldn't even properly recommend

00:03:27.679 --> 00:03:30.479
SameSite cookie as a CSRF prevention

00:03:30.479 --> 00:03:31.960
because it only worked in Chrome.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:34.220
So for four years, it was quite irrelevant.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:39.220
Then in 2020, Chrome started defaulting to lags

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.059
when the SameSite attribute was not present.

00:03:43.059 --> 00:03:46.940
And then Firefox followed up in 2021.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.740
And that's when the SameSite attribute started

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.179
become relevant.

00:03:51.179 --> 00:03:54.339
And today, it's quite a fundamental part

00:03:54.339 --> 00:03:56.899
of the client-side security.

00:03:56.899 --> 00:03:59.500
For example, in this category,

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:04.259
we had maybe reports like this one.

00:04:04.259 --> 00:04:06.500
It's also in Facebook.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.380
And that's a pretty standard post request

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.539
where the cookie has to be attached

00:04:11.539 --> 00:04:13.479
for this request to work.

00:04:13.479 --> 00:04:18.480
And with SameSite lags, a cross-site request like this

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:22.000
just wouldn't have the cookies automatically appended.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.720
There is one catch to it.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.119
It's a two-minute attack window,

00:04:26.119 --> 00:04:29.359
but more on this in the later chapter

00:04:29.359 --> 00:04:34.359
because I have a separate category for that kind of report.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:38.160
Now we'll jump to the next category

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.799
or the next two categories that I have here

00:04:41.799 --> 00:04:45.260
are where SameSite attribute was unmentioned,

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.880
but it was already in this period where it was relevant.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.200
To be precise, 11 reports

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.600
where when only Firefox would not default to lags,

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.600
when Chrome would already default to lags,

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.640
when SameSite was not present, but Firefox didn't.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.959
In this period, you could always write in the report

00:05:04.959 --> 00:05:09.320
that this CSRF would be mitigated

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.000
by the SameSite attribute,

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.799
but it still works in Firefox.

00:05:13.799 --> 00:05:16.619
And there was one report like this.

00:05:16.619 --> 00:05:18.799
It was in Atlassian.

00:05:19.679 --> 00:05:24.679
And let's not get into details of this particular bug,

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.839
but the hunter did write that due to the SameSite behavior,

00:05:28.839 --> 00:05:30.160
it doesn't work in Chrome.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.760
It only works in Firefox.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.399
And props to this hunter.

00:05:34.399 --> 00:05:39.040
He got 3.6 thousand dollars for this report

00:05:39.959 --> 00:05:44.200
and to authors of these 22 reports

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.579
which didn't mention the SameSite attribute,

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:49.239
shame on you, to be honest.

00:05:49.239 --> 00:05:54.239
I think every single CSRF report after 2020

00:05:55.459 --> 00:05:58.920
should mention the SameSite attribute.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:03.279
No matter if SameSite cookie will be appended,

00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:05.839
it won't be appended, why it won't be appended,

00:06:05.839 --> 00:06:07.920
doesn't matter which outcome it is,

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:11.000
you should always address this in your report

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.000
because it just first,

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.279
it solves a lot of problems with the triage.

00:06:16.519 --> 00:06:21.519
I'm 100% confident that in at least a few of these reports,

00:06:22.399 --> 00:06:25.600
there was a case that the report only worked in Firefox,

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:28.220
the hunter only used Firefox,

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:31.160
but the triager used Chrome and it didn't work

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:33.760
so they would go back and forth with the report.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.440
I'm 100% sure there was a case like this.

00:06:38.079 --> 00:06:40.799
If you address those things straight away,

00:06:40.799 --> 00:06:42.160
you will just get better bounties.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:45.000
You know, always when doing these case studies,

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.519
I start with the highest-paid reports

00:06:47.519 --> 00:06:49.399
and I go to the lowest-paid reports

00:06:50.399 --> 00:06:52.880
and I always, in every single case study,

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:57.880
I see the same trend that the highest-paid reports

00:06:58.519 --> 00:07:02.959
always, in general, mention every single aspect of a bug,

00:07:02.959 --> 00:07:07.239
every single prevention mechanism that could affect the bug

00:07:07.799 --> 00:07:12.799
but maybe it can be bypassed in one way or another

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.040
and then the further I go,

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:20.660
the more rare it is for hunters to mention it.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:22.559
Maybe they don't know about it.

00:07:22.559 --> 00:07:26.519
Maybe they just try to avoid the difficult conversation

00:07:26.519 --> 00:07:28.440
about the same side attribute

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:31.959
but definitely it shows that if you understand

00:07:31.959 --> 00:07:33.679
more aspects of web security,

00:07:33.679 --> 00:07:37.019
you will get bigger bounties.

00:07:38.239 --> 00:07:42.480
Then the next category, I have six reports

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.679
where it was explicitly said

00:07:45.679 --> 00:07:48.799
that the same-side cookie was none.

00:07:48.799 --> 00:07:51.160
Four of these reports are in Google

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:55.739
and one of these I did cover on my channel.

00:07:55.739 --> 00:07:59.880
It's a CSRF by David Schutz, which I really liked.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:04.880
He kind of made, exploited the TV version of YouTube

00:08:05.239 --> 00:08:08.119
to leak private videos of the victim

00:08:08.119 --> 00:08:11.119
and he got a CSRF like this.

00:08:11.119 --> 00:08:11.959
It's a great report.

00:08:11.959 --> 00:08:16.720
I will link the video and the report in the description.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:18.799
To be perfectly fair,

00:08:18.799 --> 00:08:21.579
this report could also go to the previous category

00:08:21.579 --> 00:08:24.779
because this article does not mention

00:08:24.779 --> 00:08:27.200
the same-side attribute at all

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:31.000
but I know that same-side cookie was set to none

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:35.159
because I just asked David before making the video

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:38.679
and I think because he mostly hacked on Google

00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:40.479
and Google, at least back then,

00:08:40.479 --> 00:08:43.200
used same-side none everywhere,

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:46.440
he just didn't consider same-side cookie

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:50.239
as the relevant for this report

00:08:50.239 --> 00:08:54.440
and while for me it was like, why does this work?

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:58.000
But he sent me a screenshot where it was same-side nones

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:01.000
and that's why I know about this.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:07.280
Then we have two more categories with one report each

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.520
because the bug only works in Firefox.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.239
I already have shown you.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:15.840
The first case is something

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:18.239
that maybe shouldn't be here, if I'm honest,

00:09:18.239 --> 00:09:20.080
but it's a report that I really like

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.080
and I wanted to have it in at least one of the case studies

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.919
and it wouldn't fit anywhere better than it fits to CSRFs.

00:09:29.919 --> 00:09:31.719
It's a bug by Johan Carlsson

00:09:31.719 --> 00:09:33.799
which I also covered on my channel.

00:09:33.799 --> 00:09:36.359
It's a client-side path traversal

00:09:36.359 --> 00:09:41.359
and the way it works is that in one of the places,

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:44.479
instead of a regular ID,

00:09:44.479 --> 00:09:46.719
you are injecting a dot dot slash character

00:09:46.719 --> 00:09:49.200
which is typical for path traversals

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.719
but what happens here, it's on the client-side

00:09:52.719 --> 00:09:55.159
and then when the victim would press,

00:09:55.159 --> 00:09:58.559
in this case, this button to resolve

00:09:58.559 --> 00:10:03.559
or ignore an issue, I think, or an error,

00:10:04.039 --> 00:10:07.640
then the put request would be sent

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.200
with a proper CSRF token.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:13.359
It would actually be a same-side request

00:10:13.359 --> 00:10:17.000
and because you would control the path of this request

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:21.039
and you also could inject URL query parameters

00:10:21.039 --> 00:10:25.479
and Rails is known to treat these URL query parameters

00:10:25.479 --> 00:10:30.479
pretty much the same way as the put body or the post body,

00:10:30.479 --> 00:10:31.840
you would just control the path

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:33.640
and attributes of the request

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:37.119
so you would have sort of a site-wide CSRF.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.039
The reason why I think it doesn't quite fit

00:10:41.039 --> 00:10:45.880
to the CSRF category is it ends up being a same-side request

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:49.840
and CSRF stands for cross-site request forgery

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:51.919
and this isn't really a cross-site request

00:10:51.919 --> 00:10:56.000
but I think it somehow fits to the CSRF category.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:57.919
That's why I wanted it here

00:10:57.919 --> 00:11:01.679
and one more reason for having it here

00:11:01.679 --> 00:11:04.440
is I think it is a bug class

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.919
with a lot of potential for the future

00:11:06.919 --> 00:11:09.719
because a lot of applications these days

00:11:09.719 --> 00:11:13.880
do a lot of work with the client-side JavaScript

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:18.159
and I think bugs like this will only become more common

00:11:18.159 --> 00:11:23.000
and in cases where you can't find regular CSRFs,

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:25.640
you should be looking for client-side path traversals

00:11:26.559 --> 00:11:30.239
so that's also a reason why I wanted to include this bug.

00:11:30.239 --> 00:11:34.159
If you didn't quite get it from my quick explanation now,

00:11:34.159 --> 00:11:35.440
the link to the full video

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:40.440
and the link to the report are in the description

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.320
and the last thing I have

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:46.239
but the very important thing I have

00:11:46.239 --> 00:11:51.239
is this $3,000 report in Yahoo

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:54.520
and the reason why I think it is very important

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:58.719
is because the whole video

00:11:58.719 --> 00:12:01.280
about same-side cookie defaulting to lags,

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.200
I'm saying you that if there's no same-side attribute,

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:10.320
the browser defaults as it would be same-side lags

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.880
but Chrome only does it

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:15.559
after two minutes from setting the cookie

00:12:15.599 --> 00:12:17.840
and this is very, very important

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:21.119
because it means after setting the cookie,

00:12:21.119 --> 00:12:24.440
you have the two-minute attack window

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:28.320
where the cookie works as it worked before.

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:31.559
In this case, the hunter, I'm surprised,

00:12:31.559 --> 00:12:33.320
this is the only report

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:35.679
where the hunter explicitly mentioned

00:12:35.679 --> 00:12:38.559
exploiting this two-minute attack window

00:12:39.679 --> 00:12:43.280
and the reason I mentioned it

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:47.359
is the 33 reports I showed you before

00:12:47.359 --> 00:12:51.679
that I told you that maybe these reports

00:12:51.679 --> 00:12:54.599
would be mitigated by this mechanism

00:12:54.599 --> 00:12:56.840
and they wouldn't work today.

00:12:56.840 --> 00:13:00.479
This is the only sort of scenario

00:13:00.479 --> 00:13:03.719
where you could still exploit those CSRFs

00:13:03.719 --> 00:13:05.960
and now the question you might have,

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:08.960
two minutes after the login is not a lot of time

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:09.880
and you would be right

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:13.799
but when I'm saying two minutes after setting the cookie,

00:13:13.799 --> 00:13:16.640
it is not the same as two minutes after the login

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:19.760
because cookies are not only set after the login.

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.840
You know, cookies are rotated all the time

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:25.320
and even if I'm logged into a website

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:28.359
for days, for weeks maybe,

00:13:28.359 --> 00:13:31.679
probably my cookies are being changed a lot

00:13:31.679 --> 00:13:34.679
and when you have a CSRF

00:13:34.679 --> 00:13:38.000
which does rely on this two-minute attack window,

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.479
you should also try to find scenarios

00:13:40.479 --> 00:13:43.559
where you can force the cookie to be refreshed.

00:13:43.559 --> 00:13:48.559
For example, if after opening the main page of the website,

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:51.479
maybe cookies get refreshed.

00:13:51.479 --> 00:13:56.000
Maybe if a logged in victim opens a login panel,

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:59.239
maybe then their cookies get rotated

00:13:59.239 --> 00:14:01.239
but they remain logged in.

00:14:01.239 --> 00:14:03.400
You should seek scenarios like this

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:06.239
because then you can just open

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:08.479
this two-minute attack window yourself

00:14:09.679 --> 00:14:13.559
and in this context, two minutes is a lot of time

00:14:13.559 --> 00:14:15.719
to carry out the CSRF.

00:14:15.719 --> 00:14:20.719
So I think the three most important takeaways for you

00:14:20.919 --> 00:14:23.200
from this whole video is first,

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:25.280
pay attention to GET requests.

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:28.320
If they, in one way or another,

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:32.080
can cause some changes on the server,

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:34.640
the second thing you should pay attention to

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:38.760
is the two-minute attack window in Chrome.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:40.599
I think it doesn't work like this in Firefox

00:14:40.599 --> 00:14:43.719
and I don't know for how much longer

00:14:43.719 --> 00:14:45.599
it will work like this in Chrome

00:14:46.679 --> 00:14:48.719
and the third thing is obviously,

00:14:48.719 --> 00:14:51.359
pay attention to same-site non-cookies

00:14:51.359 --> 00:14:54.320
because same-site can break some things,

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:56.320
especially if you want functionality

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:59.960
like embedding your YouTube videos on other websites.

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:01.760
Same-site breaks this functionality

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:05.760
so look for this.

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:09.599
If you're interested in more specific types of these things,

00:15:09.599 --> 00:15:12.919
check out the full case study in BBRE Premium Archive

00:15:12.919 --> 00:15:16.239
and if you are not a member of BBRE Premium yet,

00:15:16.239 --> 00:15:21.239
I recommend you this free video about IDOR case study.

00:15:22.799 --> 00:15:25.960
For now, thank you for watching and goodbye.

